WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.902
A capture comes in.

2
00:00:03.902 --> 00:00:07.065
The bus must hand it to everyone who asked for it.

3
00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:09.715
Each handler subscribes under a type.

4
00:00:09.715 --> 00:00:11.965
Ledger and receipts want captures.

5
00:00:11.965 --> 00:00:14.281
Movements wants any money movement.

6
00:00:14.281 --> 00:00:15.869
Audit wants every event.

7
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.983
Refunds wants refunds only.

8
00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:20.913
They all sit in a map, keyed by that type.

9
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:26.340
The bus from the generics workshop looks up one key: the event's own class.

10
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:26.896
Predict.

11
00:00:26.896 --> 00:00:28.215
Which handlers run?

12
00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:31.717
Only ledger and receipts.

13
00:00:31.967 --> 00:00:33.963
Movements and audit never run.

14
00:00:33.963 --> 00:00:38.423
An event's run-time class is never an interface like MoneyMovement.

15
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:41.835
So the bus works out which keys to look up.

16
00:00:41.835 --> 00:00:46.009
PaymentCaptured implements MoneyMovement, which extends PaymentEvent.

17
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:49.568
The walk starts at the event's own class: the first key.

18
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.753
Then it asks that class for its interfaces and its superclass.

19
00:00:53.967 --> 00:00:56.698
Object is not an event, so it's skipped.

20
00:00:56.698 --> 00:00:58.951
MoneyMovement is: the second key.

21
00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:01.925
Its supertype, PaymentEvent, is the third.

22
00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:03.892
Three keys, nearest first.

23
00:01:03.892 --> 00:01:07.139
Siblings like PaymentRefunded are never touched.

24
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:11.811
A class's keys never change, so the bus caches them, once per class.

25
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:16.007
Now publish visits just those keys.

26
00:01:16.007 --> 00:01:18.801
Captures first: ledger, then receipts.

27
00:01:18.801 --> 00:01:19.904
Then movements.

28
00:01:19.904 --> 00:01:20.713
Then audit.

29
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:26.725
Specific handlers run before general ones, and within one key, in subscription order.

30
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:28.634
Now audit itself.

31
00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:30.642
It's a Handler of PaymentEvent.

32
00:01:30.642 --> 00:01:36.535
Can it subscribe under PaymentCaptured, where the bus expects a Handler of PaymentCaptured?

33
00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:40.297
A handler only takes events in.

34
00:01:40.297 --> 00:01:43.321
Audit takes any event, so captures are fine.

35
00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:48.212
So Handler is declared in, and one general handler fits any subscription.

36
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:51.704
Delete the in, and the same line fails to compile.

37
00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:55.437
Java writes ? super E at each use instead.

38
00:01:55.437 --> 00:01:59.691
kotlinc puts that wildcard in for Java callers too.

39
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.877
Here is one step of the walk, in Java and Kotlin.

40
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:06.888
The rule.

41
00:02:06.888 --> 00:02:11.331
Look up the event's class, then each event supertype, nearest first.

42
00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:14.533
A handler only takes events in, so declare it in.

43
00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:15.800
Check yourself.

44
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.936
A PaymentDeclined is published.

45
00:02:17.936 --> 00:02:20.141
Which keys does the bus look up?

46
00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:23.198
Two.

47
00:02:23.198 --> 00:02:26.100
PaymentDeclined, then PaymentEvent.

48
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.011
Audit runs.

49
00:02:27.011 --> 00:02:30.659
Movements doesn't: a decline moves no money.
